# Scenario-based, condensed. Book C: Windows. Covers Ch.1-8.
DECKS=[("SC","Windows for Cyber Ops - Scenarios (Ch.1-8)",[
("mc","In a log you see powershell.exe launched with -EncodedCommand and a long Base64 string. What is happening, and why do attackers do it?",
 ["An obfuscated PowerShell command - Base64 hides keywords from simple command-line matching","A normal Windows update","A corrupted command","PowerShell being disabled"],0,
 "Ch.1: Base64 and downloaded modules are the two obfuscation methods. PowerShell is the modern shell (runs on Linux too); cmd runs a registry AutoRun at startup and keeps history."),
("mc","You get cmd.exe to open and your command runs every time - you added a value in the registry. Which, and why do attackers like it?",
 ["The Command Processor AutoRun value - quiet user-level persistence inside a trusted program","The Run key only works for PowerShell","A scheduled task","An environment variable"],0,
 "Ch.1: cmd executes AutoRun from the registry on startup."),
("mc","A domain group and a local group have the same name. A user is in the domain group - are they in the local group too?",
 ["No - different groups with different SIDs; domain membership doesn't grant local membership","Yes, same name means same group","Only if they share a RID","Only on domain controllers"],0,
 "Ch.2: SIDs identify users/groups; well-known ones (S-1-5-18=SYSTEM, RID 500=Administrator) are the same everywhere. Local data=SAM, domain data=Active Directory."),
("sa","You dumped NTLM hashes but can't crack them in time. How do you still use them, and where did they come from?",
 "Pass-the-hash: use the hash itself to authenticate instead of the password. They live in the SAM (local) and ntds.dit (domain); protected so even SYSTEM can't read them directly - you pull them as Administrator or Backup Operator.","Ch.3. Logon gives a login ID + access tokens; UAC lets you use a privileged token, controlled by registry/group policy."),
("mc","You download a flagged payload but want it to dodge the extra scrutiny Windows gives internet files. What do you do?",
 ["Ship it inside a .iso or .vhd - those containers avoid the Mark of the Web","Rename the .exe","Zip it with a password only","Nothing can avoid it"],0,
 "Ch.4: MOTW is stored in an NTFS alternate data stream (Zone.Identifier); copying to FAT32 also strips it. Windows files also have 8.3 short names and several timestamps."),
("sa","At a locked Windows login screen you press Shift 5 times and get a SYSTEM command prompt. What attack is this, what made it possible, and what defends against it?",
 "Sticky Keys attack: you took ownership of sethc.exe (via SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege/SeRestorePrivilege) and replaced it with cmd.exe. Windows Resource Protection defends against it.","Ch.5. Security descriptor = Owner, Group, DACL, SACL, Flags (Only Good Dogs Sit First). DACL=access (Door), SACL=logging (Security camera)."),
("mc","You want to see who can modify a service's files or a registry key. Which tools show the security properties/permissions?",
 ["icacls and Get-Acl, plus Sysinternals AccessChk/AccessEnum","Only File Explorer (it's always accurate)","Only regedit","Only Task Manager"],0,
 "Ch.5: File Explorer works but has subtle issues; SDDL = permissions as a string. Registry keys and values are securable objects with their own DACL (Ch.6)."),
("mc","You see svchost.exe whose parent process is winword.exe, not services.exe. Why is that a red flag, and what trick might hide it?",
 ["svchost should be a child of services.exe - an odd parent signals injection/masquerade; attackers can spoof (obfuscate) a process's parent","It's always normal","Word always launches svchost","svchost has no parent"],0,
 "Ch.7: defenders check the parent; attackers spoof it. Integrity levels (Low<Medium<High<System) drive UAC. Know the normal process tree."),
("mc","You find a service with an unquoted path 'C:\\Program Files\\My App\\svc.exe' and you can write to C:\\. How is this a privilege-escalation path?",
 ["Windows tries C:\\Program.exe first, so your planted binary runs as the service's account at startup","It isn't exploitable","Only if you're already SYSTEM","Only on domain controllers"],0,
 "Ch.8: unquoted service paths + weak permissions = escalation/persistence. Services run in the background via the Service Control Manager (sc, net, PsService, PowerShell); defined under HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services. Accounts: LocalSystem > NetworkService > LocalService."),
])]